body: |-
  THE VERB AUDIT, measured. Present: abandon absorb admit backup consolidate daemon diff fabric fork grade health hook knot lifeline log mcp objects oracle pick propose refs resolve restore scratch selvage stake status weave. Mapped against git's everyday surface, several gaps are already covered under different names: `blame` -> `lifeline` (meaning-aware, survives renames, arguably better than git's), `checkout` -> `restore`, `fsck` -> `fabric verify` + `health` (which verifies on EVERY read, not only under fsck). Genuinely ABSENT: branch, switch, clone, push, pull, fetch, tag, bisect, signing. `gc` is absent DELIBERATELY — "nothing is ever deleted" is load-bearing for recoverability per the audit.

  SO THE REPLACEMENT GAP IS THREE THINGS, not thirteen: (1) BRANCHING — a single linear selvage, no branch primitive (T-2026-07-01-017); (2) DISTRIBUTION — no clone/push/pull/fetch, which is the audit's top-line risk restated (T-2026-07-01-015, hard-deferred by Loid's A7 review); (3) SIGNING — actor is a self-asserted string (T-2026-07-01-014). Tag and bisect are conveniences. Corpus fidelity (submodules, symlinks, exec bit, CRLF, NFC filenames — T-2026-07-01-018) is a correctness tail that matters for replacement and not for this test.

  WHAT MUST BE FIXED BEFORE THE TEST, and why the list is short: Tier-0 invariant conflicts (founder call, TD-2026-08-11-663 — the failure that reaches production on a real app); the blind injection corpus (founder call, same — agent prose flows between agents in any real swarm); and KNOT CLASSIFICATION, genuine vs over-block, at resolve time. The third is not a preference: falsifiers (B) "meaning adds nothing over bytes" and (C) "failing closed is unaffordable" are both unreadable if the denominator mixes genuine contests with commuting-edit false positives, and the over-block fix was deliberately declined in favour of field observation.

  DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED BEFORE THE TEST: branching, distribution, signing, tag/bisect, corpus fidelity. Each is replacement-grade rather than test-blocking, and the reach-for-git log will rank them by measured need.

  H1 CLEARED BY TEST, not by reading. T-2026-07-01-030 claimed the guard "refuses CLEAN admits over a merged tip", serializing multi-agent work to one merge per generation at N>=3. Constructed the case on the NATIVE path: A and B fork one selvage and edit different files; A FAST_ADMITs; B admits CLEAN, producing a tip with two parents; C forks THAT tip, edits a third file, and admits — FAST_ADMIT, sealed, selvage advanced. The relaxation had already shipped (resolveMergeInput, admit.ts:562, using the merge strand's durable binding.treeId). ONE INCONSISTENCY WORTH RECORDING: the native merge strand carries `parents: 2` but `merged: false`, while the git-era guard keys on `strand.merged` — so the two paths disagree about what a merge strand IS. Harmless for a pure-native field test; a latent trap for any cross-path work.
